# Copied to .env on first run. Read by aider; also safe to `source`.
#
# Every line says `export` on purpose. aider's parser strips it, and without it
# `source .env` sets a shell variable that no program you launch can see - so
# `ollama` would ignore it and you would have no idea why.

# Where aider looks for the model.
#
# Ollama on this machine, which is where it puts itself:
export OLLAMA_API_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:11434

# A model on a lab computer, arriving over the tunnel. Not 11434, so it can
# coexist with an Ollama running here. Swap which of these two is commented:
# export OLLAMA_API_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:11435

# A machine you can reach directly (a desktop on your tailnet, say):
# export OLLAMA_API_BASE=http://gpu-box.example:11434


# Where the `ollama` command looks. A DIFFERENT variable, read by a different
# program, and aider ignores it entirely. You only need this to point `ollama
# list`, `ollama ps` or `ollama pull` at a model that is not on 11434 here:
# export OLLAMA_HOST=127.0.0.1:11435
#
# Careful: the same variable also decides what `ollama serve` BINDS to. With it
# set to 11435 on your laptop, starting a server there would land on top of the
# tunnel. Set it for inspecting a remote model, not before starting a local one.
